;; talk/institution--person.scm — objections to surfaces/institution.scm from the seat of the finance lead.

(objection 'o-institution-person-1 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-institution-signer-change :lens "person"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/board-handover.ts" "The worst day for this person is the day a signer leaves or loses a key, and the surface has no job, flow or screen for it. The leaning offers a board that still agrees with one person missing, and for anything more it says to move to a new setup. That move is the largest job the organisation will ever do here: every account, every credit line, every saving, and every counterparty must be told about a new entity, and nothing in the design serves it. The margin is also spent by the first loss: after one person is gone for good, the next absence or lost key leaves the money with nobody able to move it. The finance lead would be told at setup that replacing people is not offered, which is the one thing every organisation does within a few years."))

(objection 'o-institution-person-2 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-institution-reach :lens "person"
  :claim (guess "The leaning leaves the person with a problem that no job or screen solves. The weekend and holiday case is the ordinary case for a small organisation, and the decision's own text says payments in the short mode fail and go back to the payer while the laptops are closed. The remedy is to tell payers to use the day-long mode. The payers are customers who do not use this wallet, so the finance lead is made to explain protocol delivery modes to people they invoice, and the cost is a customer who tried to pay on Saturday, saw it fail, and blames the organisation. The surface asserts the person can be paid and that incoming payments are claimed with no vote, but admits it has not confirmed whether the organisation can receive at all while its people are offline."))

(objection 'o-institution-person-3 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-institution-silent :lens "person"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/processing/proposals.ts" "The ready-to-leave leaning has the person prepare the exit request, but a board member can have only one request waiting, and the decision itself admits a waiting request cannot be withdrawn. On the worst day, when a counterparty has stopped answering, the finance lead's slot is likely already used by this week's payment run. The board must then vote down a colleague's or their own payment run to free a slot, and the person finds out only when the exit cannot be put. The answer is a procedure about the machine's queue, not about money. Every hour spent on it is an hour added to a wait that is already twenty-five hours."))

(objection 'o-institution-person-4 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'institution-today :lens "person"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/processing/proposals.ts" "The first screen is meant to answer what is waiting for one of us, but none of the figures it shows is a waiting request, who has voted, or whether the person is blocked by their own waiting request. Those are only listed as the need institution-needs-board-state. What it does show are deadlines of payments, exits and fee notices, so the finance lead arrives and sees counterparty machinery before the one thing that unblocks their day. The screen also puts two questions into one: what needs us, and what goes wrong if we do nothing. The approver, whose whole job is one yes or no, must open the approve flow from this list of unrelated deadlines."))

(objection 'o-institution-person-5 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'institution-default-fee-cap :lens "person"
  :claim (guess "The cap is the total quoted when the run was prepared, with no margin, and the vote takes as long as two to four colleagues need, over a weekend if necessary. If fees rose in that time, the payment comes back and needs a new run, which needs a new vote. On an ordinary Friday, with a long run of suppliers, the finance lead is made to gather the same colleagues again, and the people who agree learn to approve without reading because the same run comes back. The default protects the organisation from a fee it did not see, but the person pays in repeated approval rounds and in suppliers paid late. The surface does not say how often a quote goes stale before a vote ends."))

(objection 'o-institution-person-6 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-institution-board-path :lens "person"
  :claim (guess "The refuse option turns away the most common small organisation: one owner who can move money alone, or two founders who each can. The person is told at setup to use the private surface, which has none of the jobs the institution came for (the record for an auditor, the position per counterparty, the run). The cost is that the organisation a surface is most likely to meet on its first day is sent away, and then a director is added only to meet the rule, which changes the organisation to fit the screen. Refusing is the organisation's decision to make and the surface could say plainly who can act alone, as the second option does, and still serve them."))
